 - topic: the inscribed body as a legible system for fictional cultures — the master claim is that every body code sits in one of three columns, WEALTH (assayable, alienable: jewelry as walking bank account, from Norse hacksilver and ring-giving to bead economies, inalienable heirlooms like the kula shells and pounamu, seal-rings as documents), RITE (inalienable: Polynesian tattoo with its tapu protocol, Māori moko as legal signature, Nuer gar and Yoruba ila lineage scars, Khalsa kesh, mourning hair), and PUNISHMENT (administered by authority: Chinese mò 黥 and Roman stigma, Song convict-soldier face marks, Meiji bans) — and the craft is keeping the columns distinct while designing their leaks; secondary claims: hair is the most regulable body part and therefore the state's favorite (Qing queue, 1871 danpatsu-rei, Merovingian tonsure-deposition, Mexica warrior hair); cosmetics is a vocabulary before it is a pigment (Egyptian kohl → Arabic *al-kuḥl* → English *alcohol*; Greek kosmētikē vs kommotikē; Heian oshiroi/ohaguro; sindoor); greeting and posture are engineered honorific gradients (bow degrees, wai/sampeah, proskynesis, kowtow diplomacy, Byzantine adoratio, homage's immixtio manuum) that fail legibly at embassy scenes.
 - sources: Mauss (1935/1973) "Techniques of the Body," *Economy and Society* 2; Goffman (1959) *The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life*, (1963) *Behavior in Public Places*; Bourdieu (1972/1977) *Outline of a Theory of Practice*, (1979/1984) *Distinction*; Elias (1939) *The Civilizing Process*; Hall (1966) *The Hidden Dimension*; Ekman & Friesen (1969) "The Repertoire of Nonverbal Behavior," *Semiotica* 1; Morris, Collett, Marsh & O'Shaughnessy (1979) *Gestures: Their Origins and Distribution*; Barrett, Adolphs, Marsella, Martinez & Pollak (2019) "Emotional Expressions Reconsidered," *Psychological Science in the Public Interest* 20; Rubin, ed. (1988) *Marks of Civilization* (UCLA Museum of Cultural History); Schildkrout (2004) "Inscribing the Body," *Annual Review of Anthropology* 33; Gell (1993) *Wrapping in Images: Tattooing in Polynesia* (Clarendon), (1998) *Art and Agency*; Robley (1896) *Moko; or, Māori Tattooing*; Mallon (2002) *Samoan Art and Artists* (Huia); von den Steinen (1925–28) *Die Marquesaner und ihre Kunst*; Caplan, ed. (2000) *Written on the Body: The Tattoo in European and American History* (Reaktion); Jones (1987) "Stigma: Tattooing and Branding in Graeco-Roman Antiquity," *JRS* 77; Herodotus 5.6; Juvenal 3.36; Rudenko (1970) *Frozen Tombs of Siberia*; Bede, *Historia Ecclesiastica* 3.25–26, *De temporum ratione* (725); Gregory of Tours, *Historiae*; Bodde & Morris (1967) *Law in Imperial China* (Harvard UP); Batchelor (1901) *The Ainu and Their Folk-lore*; Rhoads (2000) *Manchus and Han* (U Washington Press); Dalby (1993) *Kimono: Fashioning Culture*; Clendinnen (1991) *Aztecs: An Interpretation*; Evans-Pritchard (1940) *The Nuer*; Lienhardt (1961) *Divinity and Experience: The Religion of the Dinka*; Krige (1936) *The Social System of the Zulus*; Spencer (1988) *The Maasai of Matapato*; Titiev (1944) *Old Oraibi*; Nicholson & Shaw, eds. (2000) *Ancient Egyptian Materials and Technology* (Cambridge UP); Ovid, *Medicamina Faciei Femineae*; Tertullian, *De cultu feminarum*; Karras (1996) *Common Women* (Oxford UP); Malinowski (1922) *Argonauts of the Western Pacific*; Goody & Tambiah (1973) *Bridewealth and Dowry* (Cambridge UP); Humphrey (1985) "Barter and Economic Disintegration," *Man* 20; Einzig (1949) *Primitive Money*; Graham-Campbell & Kidd (1980) *The Vikings*; Classen (2007) *The Medieval Chastity Belt: A Myth-Making Process* (Palgrave); Bloch (1939–40) *Feudal Society*; Liutprand of Cremona, *Relatio de legatione Constantinopolitana* (968); Arrian, *Anabasis* 4.10–12; Castiglione (1528) *Il Cortegiano*; Erasmus (1530) *De civilitate morum puerilium*; Della Casa (1558) *Galateo* — triage round-1 additions (2026-10-17; web-verified): Landnámabók (Hauksbók version) ch. 268 and Eyrbyggja saga ch. 4 (oath rings reddened with sacrificial blood); Massachusetts General Court order, 15 Nov 1637 (wampum at six a penny, tender under 12d), with Davies (1994) *A History of Money* (Univ. of Wales Press); Oneida Indian Nation, "Wampum: Memorializing the Spoken Word" (accessed 2026-10-17); Aulus Gellius, *Noctes Atticae* 10.10 (after Apion) and Macrobius, *Saturnalia* 7.13 (ring finger and the heart vein); Gale, *Europe 1450–1789*, vol. 4, on the Perugia monte di pietà (1462); *Codex Theodosianus* 9.40.2 = *Codex Justinianus* 9.47.17 (Constantine, 315/6); Samadelli, Melis, Miccoli, Egarter Vigl & Zink (2015), "Complete mapping of the tattoos of the 5300-year-old Tyrolean Iceman," *Journal of Cultural Heritage* 16(5):753–758; Krutak (2012), "Tattooing among Japan's Ainu People" (1871 Hokkaidō Development Commission ban on *sinuye*); National Geographic (22 Aug 2025), "These sacred tattoos were banned in Okinawa" (*hajichi* ban, 1899); Nishiyama (2015), *Race, Biometrics, and Security in Modern Japan* (Warwick PhD), on the 1872 ban and its 1948 lifting; Namba Tomoko (2017), *Kokumin Seikatsu* (Dec. 2017), via ja.wikipedia "散髪脱刀令" (accessed 2026-10-17); Fukui Prefectural Library, Echizen gohō ikki records (Tsurug